  Terminal / View / Benchmark comparison and Terminal / View / File diff —
  read from the live file on 2026-10-18.

  WHY THESE TWO ARE IN ONE FILE

  They are the two components the terminal's rich-result frames instantiate and
  that nothing in this directory held. `479-28965.xml` captures the frames and
  shows each as a childless `<instance>`; these are what is inside them. They
  are siblings on the same parent, both 990x320, and they are read together or
  not at all, so one file follows `the-first-start-sheets.xml` rather than
  splitting a pair.

  THE CHILDLESS INSTANCES ELSEWHERE ARE A CAPTURE LIMIT, NOT A DESIGN GAP

  **This is the correction that produced this file.** Across this directory
  4718 `<instance>` elements have no children and exactly one has any, and that
  was read as the components being uncapturable — `9:2` was named as the node
  whose capture would unblock all three terminal views.

  Both halves were wrong. `get_metadata` on a live **instance** node returns its
  subtree: asked for `479:29079` it answered ten descendants. And `9:2` holds
  Button, Presence Chip, Notification / Card and the behaviour notes — no
  terminal views at all. Whoever refreshes this directory can expand any
  instance in it the same way.

  WHAT THIS FILE VOUCHES FOR, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

  Every id, name, x, y, width and height below is as `get_metadata` returned it.

  **The labels of the three `Ask alo / Action` instances are not in this file,
  because they are not in the design's layer names.** Expanding one gives a
  single text child named `Quiet`, which is the Button style variant. Read off a
  render they are **Text**, **Expand**, **Open on canvas**, left to right, in
  both components and in `488:29287`.

  **The removal marker in the diff body is not byte-verified.** It renders as a
  long centred dash aligned with the plus's crossbar and is plainly not an ASCII
  hyphen; `U+2212 MINUS SIGN` is the obvious reading and is an **inference**. A
  connector returns text, and a character retyped through an agent measures the
  retyping rather than the file — so a test wanting the exact codepoint must
  read `479:28989` in Figma. *Distinct from the addition marker, and neither is
  `U+002D`* is what was actually measured.

  THE BENCHMARK ROW IS A RULE, NOT FIVE NUMBERS

  Each row is `label(110) | 16 | bar | 16 | value(110)`, one 16 px gutter
  throughout — the label follows the bar's end, so a value pinned at x=622 is
  right for 240 ms and wrong for everything else.

  The bar is **2 px per ms**: 480 for 240, 360 for 180, both from x=126. That is
  an absolute scale and not *longest bar fills the track*, which is settled
  rather than assumed — the fixed parts take 252 of the 958-wide row leaving
  706, and the longest bar is 480, ending the content at x=732 with 226 px of
  slack.

  **Two things the design does not answer, recorded as gaps:**

  At 2 px per ms the bar overflows once a value passes **353 ms** (706 px of
  room). Clamp, rescale both bars, or wrap — the design shows 240 and 180 and
  nothing near the edge, and nobody has chosen.

  The actions disagree between these components and `488:29287` in
  `485-28999.xml`, which is the same three labels at different sizes: 100/100/160
  there against 120/120/176 here. **No rule recovers it.** The frame ratio
  990/894 = 1.1074 would give 110.7 and 177, so two of the three do not follow
  the width; and they are not hug-content, or identical labels would hug to
  identical widths. One set is stale.

  The **8 px gap** between buttons and the **48 height** agree in both and are
  safe. What differs and matters more than the widths is the layout mode: the
  row **hugs** in `488:29291` (376 wide, 216+160, no slack) and **fills** here
  (958 wide, contents ending at 432, 526 px of slack). Those are two behaviours,
  and the one a 24-language promise walks into.

  AND WHAT `485:28999` IS AND IS NOT AUTHORITATIVE FOR

  Its note defers for the **source gutter, autonomy and recovery** and for
  preserving original output and shell input. `ROOTS.md`'s headline — *485:28999
  is the contract; 479:28965 is only rendering* — is broader than the note, and
  its own next sentence scopes it to *provenance rules*. Nothing in either text
  reaches a button's width, so the disagreement above is not settled by it.
-->
